  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nAn active weather pattern returns today as a low pressure system \nmoves through the central part of the United States today and \ntomorrow. Showers and thunderstorms will be pushing through central \nand southeastern IL the next few days in multiple waves. The next \nfew days won't be a complete washout as there will be breaks in the \nrainfall. There will be a dry slot that will push into the area \novernight tonight into tomorrow morning, before additional storms \ndevelop tomorrow morning. Outside of any storms, skies will be \ncloudy. \n\nOur main concern with this system is the heavy rain potential. The \nfirst wave will enter western IL by 12-13z this morning. Between 12z \nthis morning and 12z Sunday, we are forecasting 1-2.5 inches of \nrainfall. The greatest probabilities of rainfall amounts exceeding 2 \ninches is north of I-72. The greatest rainfall amounts won't be \nwidespread and fairly localized. Some localized areas could see 4-7 \ninches of rain based off the 00z HREF LPMM accumulated \nprecipitation. WPC has us in a slight risk ERO for today and a \nmarginal risk for tomorrow. Flash flooding could occur where grounds \nare already saturated or after multiple rounds of heavy rain over \nthe same locations. \n\nThe SPC just expanded the severe threat for today to encompass our \nentire forecast area with Marginal Risk to the IL/IN border and a \nSlight Risk in place for areas southwest of a Macomb-Havana-\nTaylorville line. The primary threat today is damaging wind gusts of \n60 mph. MUCAPE will be highest in the slight risk area with values \nof 1000-2000 J/kg mid to late afternoon today. The shear values are \naround 30-40 knots. \n\nThe severe threat goes down overnight into Saturday, but MUCAPE \nvalue will remain ~1000 J/kg into Saturday. SPC does not have us \noutlooked at this time for Saturday. Although, a 2% tornado threat \ncould be introduced potentially. The low pressure center will move \ndirectly overhead on Saturday, increasing the non-supercell tornado \nparameter. Even if the low tornado chance doesn't pan out, someone \nmay see a funnel cloud(s) across central IL. Outside of \nthunderstorms, winds will be quite gusty. The HREF shows a 70-100% \nchance of wind gusts greater than 35 mph west of I-55 during the \nafternoon and evening hours. \n\nTemperatures will cool off today into Sunday with highs in the mid \n70s to low 80s. Temperatures gradually increase solidly back into \nthe 80s, nearing 90 degrees by Friday next week. Lows will be in the \n60s for the next week before warming to around 70 degrees by \nThursday night. \n\nWeather will dry out for Monday, but additional rain chances muddle \nthe new week starting Tuesday. There are low (~20%) chance for rain \noff and on throughout next week.",
